# Leaks inside a program this repo does not build.
#
# The memcheck wrap follows children, because a comptest step often reaches the
# tool under test through a shell -- so the shell is traced too, and so is
# whatever else the step runs.  bash releases neither its command table nor its
# shell-variable hash before it exits, and sed and cp keep their buffers to the
# end, so every comptest whose script runs one of them reports those leaks under
# its own name.  A leak in a program this repo does not build is not a finding
# about this repo, which is the same reason the wrap stops tracing the C++
# toolchain.
#
# Scoped to leaks on purpose.  An invalid read or write inside one of these
# programs is still reported, because that one may well be ours -- a bad buffer
# handed to a helper is our defect and not the helper's.
{
   leak-in-a-traced-program-under-usr-bin
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: all
   ...
   obj:/usr/bin/*
}
{
   leak-in-a-traced-program-under-bin
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: all
   ...
   obj:/bin/*
}
